<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arsinoe II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 316–270 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Arsinoe Philadelphos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arsinoe II was a Hellenistic queen, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I Soter</a>, who was successively the wife of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, of her half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-keraunos">Ptolemy Keraunos</a> and of her brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-ii-philadelphus">Ptolemy II Philadelphus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. As queen of Egypt she shared in all her brother's titles, acted alongside him in ritual, appeared on coinage and took part in foreign policy, a role that became a model for later Ptolemaic queens. She died in 270 or 268 BCE, and Ptolemy II made her a goddess: every temple in Egypt had to hold her cult statue, and the Fayyum became the Arsinoite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nome">nome</a>, with Arsinoe as its patron goddess.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arsinoe-ii.jpg" alt="Arsinoe II" width="330" height="325" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PHGCOM · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ArsinoeII.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
